# Keep the answer key away from the reviewer.
#
# benchmark/seeded-bugs/README.md lists every seeded bug, decoy and
# context-dependent case by file and line, and tests/test_fixture_integrity.py
# asserts each one. Both live on main, which is what makes them useful — they
# stay out of PR #1's diff.
#
# That was enough while the models could only read the diff. Once they could
# read the checkout, they found the answer key and opened it: it turned up in
# the tool logs of several measurement runs, which is a reviewer being handed
# the marking scheme. The numbers from those runs were discarded.
#
# So the key is excluded here rather than in the measurement harness. The
# harness is not the only thing that reads this repository — the live workflow
# reviews PR #1 too, and it would have found the same file.
benchmark/seeded-bugs/README.md
tests/test_fixture_integrity.py

# The measurement harness. It carries the same answer key in another form —
# a table of every bug, decoy and context case with the file and the keywords
# that identify it, including which ones are decoys and therefore which
# findings are wrong. It was missed when the two files above were excluded,
# and a model opened it on the very next run with repository access.
#
# The list in this file is no longer what the guard checks.
# `tests/test_workspace.py` searches the repository for anything speaking the
# answer key's vocabulary and asserts the models cannot reach it, because a
# hand-maintained list is exactly what let this file through.
benchmark/measure.py

# Saved findings from past measurement runs. Committed as the evidence behind
# the recorded numbers, and hidden for the same reason as the key itself: a
# transcript naming every bug is an answer key written by the model.
benchmark/runs/**
